Assume that the decays $\mathrm{n} ightarrow \mathrm{pe}^{-}  ar{
u}$ and $\Sigma^{-} ightarrow \Lambda \mathrm{e}^{-}  ar{
u}$ are governed by the same weak interaction process, and that the matrix elements describing these decays are identical. It is known that the lifetime of a free neutron is approximately $10^{3}~\mathrm{s}$. From previous estimates, we have $E_{0}(\mathrm{n} ightarrow \mathrm{pe}^{-}  ar{
u}) = 0.78 	ext{ MeV}$ and $E_{0}(\Sigma^{-} ightarrow \Lambda \mathrm{e}^{-}  ar{
u}) = 80.782 	ext{ MeV}$. Estimate the decay rate for the process $\Sigma^{-} ightarrow \Lambda \mathrm{e}^{-}  ar{
u}$.

reference_answer:
According to the decay rate formula from $\beta$ decay theory, for decays with identical matrix elements, the proportionality constant is the same:

$$
\lambda(\Sigma^{-} \rightarrow \Lambda \mathrm{e} \bar{\nu}) = \left[\frac{E_{0}(\Sigma^{-} \rightarrow \Lambda \mathrm{e}^{-} \bar{\nu})}{E_{0}(\mathrm{n} \rightarrow \mathrm{pe}^{-} \bar{\nu})}\right]^{5} \lambda_{n}
$$

Substituting the known and estimated values:

$$
\lambda(\Sigma^{-} \rightarrow \Lambda \mathrm{e}^{-} \bar{\nu}) = \left(\frac{80.782}{0.78}\right)^{5} \times 10^{-3} \approx 1.2 \times 10^{7} \text{ s}^{-1}
$$

Reference answer:
\boxed{1.2 \times 10^{7}}

