Using the properties $\sigma_{i}^{2}=1, \sigma_{x} \sigma_{y}=-\sigma_{y} \sigma_{x}=\mathrm{i} \sigma_{z}$, we have

$$
\left(\sigma_{+}\right)^{2}=\left(\sigma_{x}+\mathrm{i} \sigma_{y}\right)^{2}=\sigma_{x}^{2}-\sigma_{y}^{2}+\mathrm{i}\left(\sigma_{x} \sigma_{y}+\sigma_{y} \sigma_{x}\right)=0
$$

Similarly,

$$
\left(\sigma_{-}\right)^{2}=0
$$

Additionally,

$$
\begin{aligned}
& \sigma_{+} \sigma_{-}=\left(\sigma_{x}+\mathrm{i} \sigma_{y}\right)\left(\sigma_{x}-\mathrm{i} \sigma_{y}\right)=\sigma_{x}^{2}+\sigma_{y}^{2}-\mathrm{i}\left[\sigma_{x}, \sigma_{y}\right]=2\left(1+\sigma_{z}\right) \\
& \sigma_{-} \sigma_{+}=\left(\sigma_{x}-\mathrm{i} \sigma_{y}\right)\left(\sigma_{x}+\mathrm{i} \sigma_{y}\right)=2\left(1-\sigma_{z}\right)
\end{aligned}
$$

Therefore,

$$
\left[\sigma_{+}, \sigma_{-}\right]=4 \sigma_{z}
$$

In addition,

\begin{equation*}
\left[\sigma_{z}, \sigma_{+}\right]=\left[\sigma_{z}, \sigma_{x}\right]+\mathrm{i}\left[\sigma_{z}, \sigma_{y}\right]=2 \mathrm{i} \sigma_{y}+2 \sigma_{x}=2 \sigma_{+} \tag{1}
\end{equation*}

Taking the conjugate, we obtain

\begin{equation*}
\left[\sigma_{z}, \sigma_{-}\right]=-2 \sigma_{-} \tag{2}
\end{equation*}

Reference answer:
\boxed{[\sigma_{+}, \sigma_{-}] = 4 \sigma_{z}, [\sigma_{z}, \sigma_{+}] = 2 \sigma_{+}, [\sigma_{z}, \sigma_{-}] = -2 \sigma_{-}, (\sigma_{+})^{2} = 0, (\sigma_{-})^{2} = 0}

