Method 1: In frame $S$, when $P_{2}$ is emitted, $x=0$ and $t=\tau$. This event 1 occurs at the following location and time in frame $S^{\prime}$:

$$
\begin{gathered}
x_{1}^{\prime} = \frac{x - vt}{\sqrt{1-\left(\frac{v}{c}\right)^{2}}} = -\frac{v \tau}{\sqrt{1-\left(\frac{v}{c}\right)^{2}}} \\
t_{1}^{\prime} = \frac{t - \frac{v}{c^{2}} x}{\sqrt{1-\left(\frac{v}{c}\right)^{2}}} = \frac{\tau}{\sqrt{1-\left(\frac{v}{c}\right)^{2}}}
\end{gathered}
$$

When light pulse $P_{2}$ reaches $x^{\prime}=0$, this is event 2:

$$
\begin{gathered}
x_{2}^{\prime} - x_{1}^{\prime} = c\left(t_{2}^{\prime} - t_{1}^{\prime}\right) \\
\tau^{\prime} = t_{2}^{\prime} = t_{1}^{\prime} - \frac{x_{1}^{\prime}}{c} = \frac{\tau}{\sqrt{1-\left(\frac{v}{c}\right)^{2}}} + \frac{v}{c} \frac{\tau}{\sqrt{1-\left(\frac{v}{c}\right)^{2}}} = \tau \sqrt{\frac{1+\beta}{1-\beta}}
\end{gathered}
$$

where $\beta = \frac{v}{c}$.\\
Method 2: $\tau$ and $\tau^{\prime}$ are the time intervals between emissions of the two pulses as measured in frames $S$ and $S^{\prime}$, respectively. The light source is at $x=0$. The observer in frame $S^{\prime}$ is moving relative to the light source along their line of connection. The longitudinal Doppler effect formula can be used to find $\tau^{\prime}$.\\
$\nu = \frac{1}{\tau}$, $\nu^{\prime} = \frac{1}{\tau^{\prime}}$, where $\nu$ and $\nu^{\prime}$ are the radiation frequencies. Considering $v$ is the speed at which they are moving away from each other,

$$
\nu^{\prime} = \nu \sqrt{\frac{1-\beta}{1+\beta}}, \quad \tau^{\prime} = \tau \sqrt{\frac{1+\beta}{1-\beta}}
$$

Reference answer:
\boxed{\tau \sqrt{\frac{1+\beta}{1-\beta}}}

